WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.908
<v Examiner>Static versus dynamic equilibrium.

2
00:00:03.908 --> 00:00:04.717
<v Examiner>Four marks.

3
00:00:05.167 --> 00:00:09.028
<v Examiner>What is the difference between static and dynamic equilibrium?

4
00:00:09.028 --> 00:00:11.332
<v Examiner>How, and where, is each one detected?

5
00:00:11.782 --> 00:00:15.926
The hook: static is gravity and straight lines, in the vestibule,

6
00:00:15.926 --> 00:00:17.583
where little stones slide.

7
00:00:17.583 --> 00:00:22.555
Dynamic is spinning, in the semicircular canals, where fluid swings a gel cap.

8
00:00:23.005 --> 00:00:24.023
What they share.

9
00:00:24.023 --> 00:00:28.856
Both are sensed in the vestibular apparatus of the inner ear, by hair cells,

10
00:00:28.856 --> 00:00:30.573
which are mechanoreceptors.

11
00:00:30.573 --> 00:00:33.498
The hair cells fire when fluid, the endolymph,

12
00:00:33.498 --> 00:00:36.296
or a jelly mass moves and bends their hairs.

13
00:00:36.746 --> 00:00:40.836
Static equilibrium is the position of your head in response to gravity,

14
00:00:40.836 --> 00:00:42.565
and to straight-line movement.

15
00:00:42.565 --> 00:00:43.602
Tilting your head.

16
00:00:43.602 --> 00:00:45.272
Moving forwards or backwards.

17
00:00:45.272 --> 00:00:46.367
Going up in a lift.

18
00:00:46.367 --> 00:00:49.881
It's detected by the maculae, in the utricle and the saccule,

19
00:00:49.881 --> 00:00:52.013
which together make up the vestibule.

20
00:00:52.013 --> 00:00:55.296
The hairs there are embedded in a jelly otolith membrane,

21
00:00:55.296 --> 00:00:57.658
studded with tiny stones called otoliths.

22
00:00:57.658 --> 00:01:01.173
When you tilt your head, the stones slide and bend the hairs.

23
00:01:02.623 --> 00:01:07.800
Dynamic equilibrium is balance while your head is rotating: turning, or spinning.

24
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.293
It's detected by the crista ampullaris,

25
00:01:10.293 --> 00:01:13.745
in the ampulla at the base of each semicircular canal.

26
00:01:13.745 --> 00:01:18.283
Rotation moves the endolymph, which swings a gel cap called the cupula,

27
00:01:18.283 --> 00:01:19.881
and that bends the hairs.

28
00:01:20.331 --> 00:01:24.875
Both send impulses along the vestibular branch of cranial nerve eight,

29
00:01:24.875 --> 00:01:27.276
to the brain stem and the cerebellum.

30
00:01:27.276 --> 00:01:31.041
And signals that fire with no real movement cause vertigo.

31
00:01:32.491 --> 00:01:33.127
The traps.

32
00:01:33.127 --> 00:01:38.023
Static is the vestibule: the utricle, the saccule, the maculae, the otoliths.

33
00:01:38.023 --> 00:01:42.602
Dynamic is the semicircular canals: the ampulla, the crista, the cupula.

34
00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:44.764
Swap them and you lose both marks.

35
00:01:44.764 --> 00:01:47.753
Second: static doesn't mean no movement at all.

36
00:01:47.753 --> 00:01:51.314
A lift going up is static, because it's a straight line.

37
00:01:51.314 --> 00:01:52.777
Dynamic means rotation.

38
00:01:52.777 --> 00:01:55.765
Third: the cochlea is for hearing, not balance.

39
00:01:56.215 --> 00:01:56.855
<v Examiner>Once more.

40
00:01:56.855 --> 00:01:58.839
<v Examiner>Static and dynamic equilibrium.

41
00:02:01.289 --> 00:02:05.771
Both are sensed by hair cells in the vestibular apparatus of the inner ear,

42
00:02:05.771 --> 00:02:09.356
and sent along nerve eight to the brain stem and cerebellum.

43
00:02:09.356 --> 00:02:14.375
Static equilibrium is head position relative to gravity, and straight-line movement.

44
00:02:14.375 --> 00:02:17.721
It's detected by the maculae in the utricle and saccule,

45
00:02:17.721 --> 00:02:20.171
where otoliths slide when the head tilts.

46
00:02:20.171 --> 00:02:22.979
Dynamic equilibrium is balance during rotation.

47
00:02:22.979 --> 00:02:26.922
It's detected by the crista ampullaris in each semicircular canal,

48
00:02:26.922 --> 00:02:30.388
where the endolymph swings the cupula and bends the hairs.
